.he "s (1)                        8/12/26                        s (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
s
- a screen editor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
s file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
S is a small screen editor in the manner of vi.  It takes exactly one
file, which it reads into a buffer; the file on disk is not touched
until a write is asked for.
.sp
A command is a letter, most of which may be given a repeat count typed
in front of it.  The three operators c, d and y - change, delete and
yank - are followed by an address, and act on everything between the
cursor and where that address lands.  An address given on its own just
moves the cursor there, and an operator typed twice over takes the
whole line.
.sp
The editor is in command mode until a command that takes text puts it
into insert mode, which lasts until escape.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ADDRESSES
.in +5
.br
.nf
     h l       one character left, right
     backspace one character left
     j k       one line down, up
     space     one character right
     w b       forward, back one word
     0 $       the first, last character of a line
     f F       forward, back to a character
     ; \       repeat, reverse that search
     / n       search for a pattern, and repeat it
     G         a line by number, the last by default
     H M L     the top, middle, bottom line shown
     -         a line by number, relative
     ' `       the marked line, the marked character
     cc dd yy  the whole line
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     a A i     append after, at end of line, insert
     o O       open a line below, above
     C D       change, delete to end of line
     x         delete characters
     r s       replace, substitute one character
     J         join this line and the next
     p P       put the yank buffer after, before
     u         undo
     m         mark the cursor, for ' ` and *
     .         do the last change again
     *         make one change everywhere, to the mark
     #         fill the lines from here to the mark
     ?         the file name, and where you are
     q         quit, asking first if modified
     ZZ        write if modified, and quit
     :         an input or output command
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COLON COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     :w        write, with an address and a name
     :e        edit another file
     :r        read a file in after this line
     :R        read a file in at this line
.fi
.sp
A colon command with no file name uses the file being edited.  One
that would lose a modified buffer asks first.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The join command puts one space between the two lines and throws away
the leading blanks of the second.
.sp
The star command works from the cursor to the mark, alternating a
repeat of the last search with a repeat of the last change, which is
how one change is made everywhere it applies.  It stops after fifty
changes and says so; typing it again carries on from there.
.sp
The fill command reflows the lines from the cursor to the mark, to
seventy-eight columns unless a count says otherwise.
.sp
S is Webb Miller's editor from A Software Tools Sampler, Prentice-Hall
1987, by way of the HI-TECH C port.  The buffer and the screen are
each behind a manager, so the part that knows about the terminal is
small.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
